# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Oriant — environment template
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
#  YOU CAN BUILD AND TEST ALL OF M1-M3 WITH AN EMPTY FILE.
#
#  Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in ONLY what the milestone you are
#  on requires. Every variable below is blank on purpose; a blank variable is
#  a supported state, not a broken one.
#
#      cp .env.example .env.local
#
#  `.env.local` is gitignored. This template carries NAMES ONLY, never values.
#  Every variable here is server-side. None of them may be given a
#  NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix — that prefix inlines the value into the browser bundle.
#
#  Full guide, including which milestone needs what: docs/RUNTIME_SETUP.md
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  A. RUNTIME MODE, STORAGE AND THE SCHEDULER
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ORIANT_RUNTIME_MODE — the single switch that decides whether the agent
# runtime talks to anything outside this process.
#
#   fixture (DEFAULT)  FixtureReasoner + StubToolClient. No network calls and no
#                      keys. Paired with the sandbox's FixedClock and seeded
#                      newId() it is fully deterministic: same input, same
#                      events, same verdict, every time. (Fixture mode alone
#                      removes the network; the clock and id factory are what
#                      remove the remaining variance.) THIS IS THE IMPORTANT ONE —
#                      EVERYTHING IN M1, M2 AND M3 WORKS WITH NO KEYS AT ALL.
#                      Runs, approvals, policy decisions, limit breaches,
#                      pause/resume and sandbox verdicts are all exercised
#                      here. You do not need a single credential to develop,
#                      test or demo the runtime spine.
#
#   live               Real LLM for `reason` steps and real tool clients for
#                      `fetch`/`act` steps. Requires at minimum the AI& block
#                      below, plus whichever tool credentials the workflow
#                      touches.
#
# Required?      No. Unset behaves exactly as `fixture`, and any value other
#                than `live` is treated as `fixture` (fail closed).
# First needed:  M1, and only if you want live reason steps. Never needed for
#                fixture work.
# Breaks without it: nothing.
# Where from:    you. It is a local choice, not a credential.
#
# Note: this is the RUNTIME's switch (lib/runtime, Role C). It is separate from
# the older per-provider fallback in lib/server/providers/env.ts, which powers
# the /demo lane and independently drops to fixture whenever a provider's keys
# are absent. The two do not conflict; they govern different lanes.
ORIANT_RUNTIME_MODE=

# ORIANT_RUNTIME_STORAGE — where the runtime keeps what it learns.
#
#   file (DEFAULT)     Unset, blank, or "file". One JSON file per record under
#                      data/runtime/, written atomically. Runs, events,
#                      approvals, decisions, packages, triggers, queued jobs,
#                      deployments and agent state all outlive the process —
#                      which is what makes the approval interrupt real, because
#                      a run that pauses for four hours must not depend on the
#                      process it paused in still being alive.
#
#   memory             The in-memory stores. Correct, fast, and DISCARDED WITH
#                      THE PROCESS: a paused run and its pending approval are
#                      gone on restart. Reach for it for a throwaway experiment,
#                      never for anything you intend to come back to.
#
#   anything else      THROWS at session construction. Note that this is the
#                      opposite posture to ORIANT_RUNTIME_MODE above, which
#                      shrugs at a value it does not know and picks fixture. The
#                      asymmetry is deliberate: for the reasoner the dangerous
#                      default is "does something", for storage it is "forgets
#                      something". "postgres" is the value somebody will type
#                      the day docs/STORAGE.md §7 is taken up, and silently
#                      reading it as "file" would hand them a green server that
#                      is not using the database they just configured.
#
# Required?      No, and the DEFAULT IS THE DURABLE ONE — you have to ask for
#                the forgetful one by name.
# First needed:  never, to make anything work. M4 onwards depends on durability,
#                and durability is what you get for free.
# Breaks without it: nothing.
# Where from:    you. A deployment choice, not a credential.
ORIANT_RUNTIME_STORAGE=

# ORIANT_RUNTIME_DATA_DIR — where "file" storage puts those records.
#
# Required?      No. Blank means <working directory>/data/runtime. A relative
#                value is resolved against the working directory; an absolute
#                one is taken as given, and absolute is what two processes
#                started from different directories need — otherwise each gets
#                its own private copy of every run and neither sees the other's
#                queue claims.
# First needed:  never. Set it to keep runtime state outside the repo, or to
#                give two checkouts separate state.
# Breaks without it: nothing. data/ is already gitignored, so nothing is
#                committed either way.
# Where from:    you. One caution: this directory is NOT encrypted and NOT
#                access-controlled. Run context holds customer names, invoice
#                amounts and draft email bodies, and it inherits the
#                filesystem's permissions and nothing more — so do not point it
#                at a synced or shared folder (docs/STORAGE.md §6).
ORIANT_RUNTIME_DATA_DIR=

# ORIANT_POLLER — whether this server process turns the scheduler by itself.
#
#   off (DEFAULT)      Unset, blank, "off", "false", "0", "no" or "disabled".
#                      Triggers still register at activation, agents still read
#                      as live, and every schedule still computes its next fire
#                      time — but NOTHING FIRES ON ITS OWN. Each firing waits
#                      for a POST to /api/runtime/scheduler, which is one pass
#                      of the same worker.
#
#   on                 Also "true", "1", "yes", "enabled". A background poller
#                      runs one worker pass every ORIANT_POLLER_INTERVAL_MS:
#                      due schedules are queued, jobs are claimed, runs start
#                      with nobody watching. This is the switch that turns an
#                      activated plan into a workforce that runs itself.
#
# Required?      No, and OFF IS THE DEFAULT ON PURPOSE — it is the only variable
#                here that makes the process act without being asked. `npm run
#                dev` should serve screens, not start dispatching a workforce
#                thirty seconds later against whatever credentials happen to be
#                in .env.local. In live mode that would be real customer email.
#                Turning it on is a decision somebody writes down.
# First needed:  M4, and only for a deployment that is meant to fire schedules
#                unattended. Never needed to develop, test or demo: the verify
#                suite drives the worker directly and the route drives one pass
#                per request.
# Breaks without it: nothing breaks — a live workforce simply never starts a
#                run on its own. That is the failure worth naming, because it is
#                silent: the Friday sweep does not happen and nobody notices
#                until the invoices are a month old.
# Where from:    you. It is a deployment choice, not a credential.
#
# Anything this build cannot read — a typo like "treu" — is REFUSED rather than
# guessed at: the poller does not start, the server still boots, and the reason
# is printed on stderr with the accepted values beside it. A mistyped switch
# that quietly meant "off" would be the silent failure above wearing a green
# deployment. Details, including what to do when several server processes share
# one queue: docs/RUNTIME_SETUP.md §3b.
ORIANT_POLLER=

# ORIANT_POLLER_INTERVAL_MS — milliseconds between passes, e.g. 5000.
#
# Required?      No. Blank means 30000 (30s), which is the right order of
#                magnitude for cron: a TriggerSpec schedule is minute-granular,
#                so polling faster buys latency on a demo and nothing else.
#                Whole milliseconds only, and values under 1000 are refused —
#                below a second is a busy loop against the job store, and is far
#                more likely to be seconds typed into a milliseconds field.
#                Read only when ORIANT_POLLER is on.
ORIANT_POLLER_INTERVAL_MS=


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  B. AI&  —  the agent brain
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# What it is: an OpenAI-compatible chat-completions endpoint. It powers every
# `reason` step in a workflow — deciding which overdue invoices to chase,
# drafting the reminder text, judging whether a thread needs a human. The
# runtime never lets the model choose a tool: control flow stays in the
# executor and the model is confined to `reason` steps.
#
# Required?      REQUIRED for live mode. Irrelevant in fixture mode.
# First needed:  M1 (runtime core), the moment you set ORIANT_RUNTIME_MODE=live.
# Breaks without it: in live mode the AiAndReasoner constructor throws
#                ReasonerConfigError, naming the missing variables, before any
#                run starts — loud at wiring rather than silent at the first
#                `reason` step. Fixture mode is completely unaffected;
#                FixtureReasoner returns canned, deterministic results.
# Where from:    your AI& console. Base URL and model id are org-specific;
#                model access varies per organisation, so copy both from the
#                console rather than guessing.
#
# All three must be present together. One or two of the three is treated as
# not configured.
AIAND_API_KEY=
AIAND_BASE_URL=
AIAND_MODEL=


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  C. Doubleword  —  agent package generation
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# What it is: async/batch OpenAI-compatible inference used by the Agent Factory
# to compile an `AgentSpec` into an `AgentPackage` (system prompt, per-workflow
# prompt fragments, resolved tool bindings, flattened operation allowlist).
#
# Required?      OPTIONAL. The default Factory path is a deterministic LOCAL
#                compiler — no model, no network, stable checksums. That local
#                path is what the M2 equivalence test measures against, so it
#                stays supported permanently.
# First needed:  M2, and only if you want generation to be model-driven.
# Breaks without it: model-driven generation is unavailable. Packages are still
#                produced by the local compiler and still execute on the M1
#                runtime. Nothing in M2's exit criterion depends on this key.
# Where from:    your Doubleword console. If the console shows different async
#                endpoint paths, adjust the constants at the top of
#                lib/server/providers/doubleword.ts.
#
# All three must be present together.
DOUBLEWORD_API_KEY=
DOUBLEWORD_BASE_URL=
DOUBLEWORD_MODEL=


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  D. Daytona  —  sandbox isolation
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# What it is: a remote sandbox that a scenario is executed INSIDE. The runner is
# bundled (npm run sandbox:bundle), uploaded, and run there; the ScenarioResult
# comes back over stdout. Nothing about the scenario runs on this machine, which
# is the only version of "isolation" worth the name — wrapping a local call in a
# remote handle would report a remote verdict for local work.
#
# Required?      OPTIONAL, and OPT-IN. Nothing reads these unless a caller asks
#                for the Daytona isolation by name. `npm run verify` and
#                `npm run verify:m3` stay in process on purpose: the M3 exit
#                criterion is a byte-identical verdict across five consecutive
#                runs, and 24 network round trips per run would make that slow
#                and hostage to a DNS hiccup.
# First needed:  M3, and only when you want the isolation guarantee — a future
#                generator that emits executable code, or proving to someone
#                else that nothing escaped.
# Breaks without it: you lose ISOLATION, nothing else. Scenario execution,
#                approval checkpoints, pass rates and verdicts all work
#                locally. Determinism does NOT depend on this key — it comes
#                from the injected Clock, seeded newId() and stubbed tools, and
#                a remotely executed scenario is byte-identical to a local one
#                for exactly that reason. If it ever is not, that is a bug in
#                isolation, not a tolerance to widen.
# Where from:    app.daytona.io → Keys. The key needs sandbox CREATE and DELETE
#                permission; without create it authenticates fine and then fails
#                403 on the first sandbox. DAYTONA_API_URL defaults to
#                https://app.daytona.io/api and only needs setting for a
#                self-hosted or regional endpoint.
#
# One other thing reads DAYTONA_API_KEY, and it can no longer use it. The legacy
# /demo lane's validator (lib/server/providers/daytona.ts → ValidateScreen)
# hand-rolled its own transport, and the endpoint it called now 404s: the toolbox
# moved behind proxy.app.daytona.io, which rejects an organisation key. That
# adapter no longer attempts the call — it runs its checks locally, labels them
# `fixture`, and says on the manifest that this was not a sandbox run. Setting
# the key does not give the /demo lane isolation; only lib/runtime/sandbox/remote
# has that, and only when a caller asks for it.
#
# Check it before you rely on it:  npm run daytona:check
# That creates and deletes one real sandbox, prints presence-and-length for the
# key (never the key), and exits non-zero with one actionable sentence when
# isolation is unavailable. The two failures a fresh organisation hits are named
# there exactly: "no default region" (400) and "Access denied" (403).
DAYTONA_API_KEY=
DAYTONA_API_URL=

# DAYTONA_TARGET — the region to place sandboxes in, e.g. "us".
#
# Required?      No. Leave it blank and the organisation's default region is
#                used. Set it only to override that, or as the quick way past a
#                400 "This organization does not have a default region" on a
#                brand-new org — though setting the default in the Daytona
#                Dashboard is the fix that stays fixed.
DAYTONA_TARGET=


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  E. TOOL / INTEGRATION CREDENTIALS
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
#  LEAVE EVERY VARIABLE IN THIS SECTION BLANK UNTIL ROLE D'S INTEGRATION LAYER
#  LANDS. The stub tool client covers every operation listed below in fixture
#  mode, with believable shaped responses. Nothing in M1-M3 needs any of them.
#
#  These credentials are OWNED by Role D's integration layer and merely
#  CONSUMED by the runtime, at run time, through getToolClient(integrationId).
#  Role C code must never read them directly.
#
#  Required?      OPTIONAL until you point live mode at real customer systems.
#  First needed:  M4 (Activation), when a real agent acts on a real account.
#  Breaks without them: in live mode, any `fetch`/`act` step touching that
#                 integration fails; the integration reads as not connected on
#                 the Activation checklist and blocks go-live for agents that
#                 require it. Fixture mode is unaffected.
#
#  A credential NEVER widens what an agent may do. The package's
#  `allowedOperations` and the agent's `AgentPolicy` decide that, and they are
#  checked before any client is called. Supplying a HubSpot token does not let
#  an agent issue a refund if policy forbids it.
#
#  Operation ids below come from lib/plan/operations.ts, the single shared
#  vocabulary. If an operation is not in that registry, no key enables it.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── Google (one OAuth client covers three integrations) ────────────────────
#
# Unlocks:
#   gmail                  gmail.threads.read · gmail.messages.read
#                          gmail.drafts.create · gmail.messages.send
#   google-calendar        google-calendar.events.list
#                          google-calendar.availability.read
#                          google-calendar.events.create
#                          google-calendar.events.update
#   google-drive           google-drive.files.read
#
# Scopes to request, roughly: gmail.readonly, gmail.compose, gmail.send,
# calendar.events, drive.readonly. Grant only what the agents actually use.
#
# Where from:  Google Cloud console → APIs & Services → Credentials → OAuth 2.0
#              client ID (Web application). Enable the Gmail, Calendar and
#              Drive APIs on the same project.
# Note:        GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI must match the callback registered on the
#              client exactly. The final callback path is Role D's to define,
#              so this template leaves it blank rather than inventing a route.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=

# ── HubSpot ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Unlocks: hubspot.contacts.read · hubspot.deals.read · hubspot.invoices.list
#          hubspot.notes.create · hubspot.invoices.write_off
#          hubspot.refunds.issue
#
# The last two are high-risk writes. They are gated by AgentPolicy
# (alwaysApprove / forbidden) regardless of this token — the Finance agent
# pauses on a write-off and refuses a refund outright even with a valid token.
#
# Where from:  HubSpot → Settings → Integrations → Private Apps → create an
#              app with the CRM scopes you need, then copy its access token.
HUBSPOT_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# ── WhatsApp Business ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Unlocks: whatsapp-business.messages.read · whatsapp-business.messages.send
#
# Where from:  Meta for Developers → your WhatsApp Business app. The phone
#              number id is on the WhatsApp → API Setup panel; the access token
#              is a system-user token from Business Settings (temporary tokens
#              expire in hours and are not suitable beyond a first smoke test).
WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# ── QuickBooks ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Unlocks: quickbooks.invoices.list · quickbooks.payments.read
#
# Read-only today. The registry defines no QuickBooks write operation, so this
# credential cannot cause a financial side effect; money movement is not part
# of the vocabulary.
#
# Where from:  Intuit developer portal → your app → Keys & credentials.
#              Sandbox and production keys are distinct; use sandbox first.
QUICKBOOKS_CLIENT_ID=
QUICKBOOKS_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── Slack ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Unlocks: slack.messages.read · slack.messages.post  (internal channels only)
#
# Where from:  api.slack.com/apps → your app → OAuth & Permissions → Bot User
#              OAuth Token (starts xoxb-). Needs the channel read/write scopes
#              for the channels the agents post to.
SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=

# ── Mailchimp ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Unlocks: mailchimp.campaigns.read · mailchimp.campaigns.create_draft
#          mailchimp.campaigns.publish
#
# The Marketing agent runs in draft_only mode, so in practice it uses the read
# and draft operations; publishing is a high-risk write behind approval.
#
# Where from:  Mailchimp → Account → Extras → API keys. The server prefix is
#              the suffix of your key and the subdomain of your dashboard URL
#              (e.g. "us14").
MAILCHIMP_API_KEY=
MAILCHIMP_SERVER_PREFIX=


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  F. DATABASE
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# DATABASE_URL — the Postgres connection string for the production storage
# path: runs, run events, approvals, packages, deployments, schedules and agent
# runtime config.
#
# Required?      NO, AND NOTHING IN THIS BUILD READS IT. Durability arrived as
#                the file-backed stores under data/runtime/ — see
#                ORIANT_RUNTIME_STORAGE in section A — which is the default and
#                needs no service, no migration and no setup step.
#                `runtimeStorage()` implements exactly "file" and "memory" and
#                throws on anything else, "postgres" included.
# First needed:  when the migration in docs/STORAGE.md §7 is taken up. Step 1
#                there is adding the arm that reads this variable.
# Breaks without it: nothing, today. It is named here because it is the decided
#                production path and the migration doc points at it — but
#                SETTING IT CHANGES NO BEHAVIOUR WHATSOEVER, which is worth
#                knowing before anyone concludes from a green deployment that
#                runs are going into a database.
# Where from:    your own Postgres (local, Docker, or hosted), when that day
#                comes. Use a separate database from any demo data.
#
# The legacy /demo lane keeps using the data/db.json file store and ignores
# this variable too.
DATABASE_URL=


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  G. WHICH SCREEN EACH OPERATE ROUTE RENDERS
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Five routes under /app/workspace have two screens behind them: the SCRIPTED
# demo screen that has always been there, and a live one that reads the real
# runtime through /api/runtime/*. The scripted lane is the default and stays
# supported permanently — it is the fallback that carries the demo if another
# lane slips (ROLE_C_PLAN, "keep the fixture path working permanently").
#
#   blank / demo (DEFAULT)  the scripted screen, exactly as it shipped
#
#   live                    the runtime-backed screen for that one route, for
#                           this deployment, for everyone who opens it
#
#   anything else           REFUSED. The page renders a refusal naming the
#                           variable, the value and the accepted forms rather
#                           than falling back — falling back would put a
#                           scripted screen, whose controls change nothing, in
#                           front of somebody who asked in writing for the live
#                           one, and the scripted screens are convincing.
#
# You do not need these to see a live screen. `?live=1` on the URL opts one page
# over without touching this file, and `?live=0` forces the demo back even where
# the variable says live — which is how a deployment that has switched over
# still opens the scripted screen for a rehearsal. The query parameter wins in
# both directions; the whole rule lives once in components/live/lane.ts.
#
# Required?      No. All five blank is the shipped behaviour.
# First needed:  M5 for workspace and approvals, M6 for calendar, agents and
#                integrations — and only for a deployment that wants the live
#                screens without a query string on every link.
# Breaks without it: nothing breaks. The sidenav leads to the scripted screens,
#                which work. But an owner reading them is reading a narrative
#                rather than their own workforce, and the two look alike; that
#                is precisely why switching a route over is something a person
#                types rather than something the code infers.
# Where from:    you. A deployment choice, not a credential. These name a screen
#                and nothing else, and they are still never given a
#                NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix — the server layout resolves them and hands
#                the shell the lane names, so the set of values that can reach a
#                browser stays enumerated in one place.
ORIANT_WORKSPACE_LANE=
ORIANT_APPROVALS_LANE=
ORIANT_CALENDAR_LANE=
ORIANT_AGENTS_LANE=
ORIANT_INTEGRATIONS_LANE=


# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  H. NOT USED BY THE RUNTIME
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# NOSANA_* belongs to YJ's discovery/voice lane (Whisper transcription on the
# kickoff call, /demo). The Role C runtime never reads these, and setting them
# changes no runtime behaviour whatsoever. Leave them blank unless you are
# working on that lane.
#
# If you are: NOSANA_WHISPER_URL is a deployment output, the service URL you
# get after deploying a Nosana job — and it must be a job that SERVES AN HTTP
# ASR API (multipart audio in, JSON with a `text` field out). A notebook-style
# template answers with an HTML page and 403.
NOSANA_WHISPER_URL=
NOSANA_API_KEY=
#
# NEXT_DIST_DIR is set automatically by scripts/next-prod.mjs so that
# `npm run build` and `npm run dev` use separate output directories. Do not set
# it by hand.
#
# NEXT_RUNTIME and NEXT_PHASE are Next's own, substituted at build time, and
# instrumentation.ts reads them to decide whether the scheduler poller may start
# — Node.js server runtime only, and never during `next build`, because a poller
# started by a build would execute real runs as a side effect of compiling the
# site. They are listed here only so nobody sets them by hand looking for that
# switch; the switch is ORIANT_POLLER in section A.
